<attribute name="mainDescription" nodeId="951ce262">&lt;p&gt;
    Project roles are expected behavior associated with a specific job or status of an individual or group participating in
    the project. Each individual/group will be expected to act within the scope and content of their assigned role. This
    enables clarity, speed and predictability when collaboration between people is needed throughout the project life
    cycle.
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    Project responsibilities are the duties or obligations an individual or group needs to perform to complete an assigned
    task or job, while acting within the scope and content of the related role. Project responsibilities specify respective
    roles and authorities. Responsibilities will vary from project to project
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
<attribute name="externalId" nodeId="aee1f67c"></attribute>
<attribute name="keyConsiderations" nodeId="b73f47df"></attribute>
<attribute name="longPresentationName" nodeId="b945e1a2"></attribute>
<attribute name="purpose" nodeId="6cc0fa30">&lt;p&gt;
    The project roles and responsibilities work product helps to identify how project roles are involved
    with each activity in a project. Charting project roles and responsibilities is a systematic and highly
    participative technique to:
&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Identify all functions (activities, tasks and decisions) that have to be accomplished for effective operation.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Clarify roles and individual levels of participation in relation to each activity.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Gain agreement and “buy-in” from the project team on who should be doing what.
    &lt;/li&gt;
    &lt;li&gt;
        Align stakeholders to their accountabilities on the project.
    &lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;
    The project roles and responsiblities work product allows the EY project managers to understand,
    establish and communicate roles and responsibilities. It helps identify gaps and redundancies and is used as a basis to
    agree and communicate who does what in a process.
&lt;/p&gt;</attribute>
